# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-basin/blob/main/src/functions/Stable2.sol#L74-L103


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The accuracy of the calculation is not correct because `calcLpTokenSupply()` does not revert even though it has not converged.

## Proof of Concept
According to sponsor team:
> the assumption is that if the function has not converged within 255, then the value is invalid. It may certainly be close, but given that in most cases it should converge within 4 loops, we prefer it to break rather than return an inaccurate value.

However, as a result of multiple simulations, we can see that `calcLpTokenSupply()` does not converge in many cases.
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-basin/blob/main/src/functions/Stable2.sol#L74-L103
This is simualation result.

>in case (reserve0, reserve1) = (600, 800), result is that: i = 254, return value = 1.3952e+21
>in case (reserve0, reserve1) = (700, 800), result is that: i = 254, return value = 1.4989e+21

But there exists `revert()` in `calcReserve()`.
```solidity
    function calcReserve(
        uint256[] memory reserves,
        uint256 j,
        uint256 lpTokenSupply,
        bytes memory data
    ) public view returns (uint256 reserve) {
        uint256[] memory decimals = decodeWellData(data);
        uint256[] memory scaledReserves = getScaledReserves(reserves, decimals);

        // avoid stack too deep errors.
        (uint256 c, uint256 b) = getBandC(a * N * N, lpTokenSupply, j == 0 ? scaledReserves[1] : scaledReserves[0]);
        reserve = lpTokenSupply;
        uint256 prevReserve;

        for (uint256 i; i < 255; ++i) {
            prevReserve = reserve;
            reserve = _calcReserve(reserve, b, c, lpTokenSupply);
            // Equality with the precision of 1
            // scale reserve down to original precision
            if (reserve > prevReserve) {
                if (reserve - prevReserve <= 1) {
                    return reserve / (10 ** (18 - decimals[j]));
                }
            } else {
                if (prevReserve - reserve <= 1) {
                    return reserve / (10 ** (18 - decimals[j]));
                }
            }
        }
@       revert("did not find convergence");
    }
```
As a result, the accuracy of the calculation is not correct because `calcLpTokenSupply()` does not revert even though it has not converged.
This problem also appears in other functions, such as `calcReserveAtRatioSwap()` and `calcReserveAtRatioLiquidity()`.

## Tools Used
Mannual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Add `revert()` to `calcLpTokenSupply()`.


